HY='THE NĀRĀYAṆĪ STUTI'
V=[
VP(C,'2–3',S('2','3'),'FACES LIKE OPEN LOTUSES',
 sc('Agni leading the gods in praise, their faces glowing like opening lotuses, before the Goddess Katyayani',FLOWERS(6)+DEVI(110,0,.65,False)+''.join(GOD(30+k*40,150,.45,*c) for k,c in enumerate([('#ff8c1a','#e0a800'),('#ffd60a','#c98b5a'),('#fff','#9fd3f0'),('#2a9d6f','#c98b5a'),('#c0392b','#c98b5a')]))+''.join(MOUNT('lotus',30+k*40,104,.35) for k in range(5))),
 'When the great lord of asuras was slain there by the Devī, the gods with Indra, led by Agni, praised Kātyāyanī, their wish fulfilled, their faces shining like open lotuses, lighting up the quarters: “O Devī, remover of the suffering of those who take refuge in you, be gracious. Be gracious, Mother of the whole world. Be gracious, Ruler of the universe; protect the universe. You are the Ruler, O Devī, of all that moves and does not move.”',
 [('vahni-purogamāḥ','<b>led by Agni</b>, the fire god'),('kātyāyanī','<b>Kātyāyanī</b>: a name of Durgā'),('vikāsi-vaktra-abja','<b>faces like opening lotuses</b>'),('prapanna-ārti-hare','<b>remover of the pain of those who surrender</b>'),('prasīda','<b>be gracious!</b>')],
 '<b>The last great hymn.</b> The <b>Nārāyaṇī stuti</b> is named after its refrain, “nārāyaṇi namo’stu te”. Fire, the god who carries every offering, leads the singing. The first word they repeat is <i>prasīda</i>: be gracious, be pleased.',
 'The final hymn begins: <b>“Prasīda, Mother of the world.”</b>'),
VP(C,'4–5',S('4','5'),'EARTH, WATER AND MĀYĀ',
 sc('The Goddess as the ground beneath the world and the water nourishing it; a seed of the universe glowing in her hand',HIMA+'<path d="M0 120 h220 V160 H0z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/><path d="M0 130 q55 -6 110 0 t110 0" fill="none" stroke="#4aa3df" stroke-width="5"/>'+DEVI(110,0,.6,False)+GLOW(150,60,12)+'<circle cx="150" cy="60" r="6" fill="#2a9d6f" stroke="#111"/>'),
 'You alone are the support of the world, for you abide in the form of the earth. By you, abiding in the form of the waters, this whole world is nourished, O you of unsurpassable valour. You are the power of Viṣṇu, of endless valour; you are the seed of the universe, the supreme Māyā. All this world, O Devī, is deluded by you; you, when pleased, are the cause of liberation on earth.',
 [('ādhāra-bhūtā','<b>the support</b>'),('mahī-svarūpa','<b>in the form of the earth</b>'),('āpyāyate','<b>is nourished</b>'),('vaiṣṇavī śakti','<b>the power of Viṣṇu</b>'),('viśvasya bījam','<b>the seed of the universe</b>'),('mukti-hetuḥ','<b>the cause of liberation</b>')],
 '<b>The whole text in two verses.</b> She holds the world as earth and feeds it as water. She is Māyā who deludes it, and, when pleased, the one who frees it. That was Medhas’s first answer to the king, back in chapter 1.',
 'She binds the world as Māyā, <b>and frees it when pleased</b>.'),
VP(C,'6–7',S('6','7'),'EVERY WOMAN IS HER FORM',
 sc('Women of every age and walk of life, a scholar, a mother, a farmer, a girl, each with a small glow of the Goddess',''.join(person(24+k*43,150,c,'#c98b5a',.75)+GLOW(24+k*43,96,10) for k,c in enumerate(['#c0392b','#e05a8a','#2a9d6f','#ffd60a','#9b5de5']))+DEVI(110,0,.35,False)+T(110,24,'striyaḥ samastāḥ',11)),
 'All forms of knowledge are aspects of you, O Devī, and so are all women in all the worlds. By you alone, the Mother, this world is filled. What praise can there be for you, who are beyond the highest and lowest words of praise? When you are everything, the Devī who grants enjoyment and liberation, what words, however lofty, could serve to praise you?',
 [('vidyāḥ samastāḥ','<b>all forms of knowledge</b>'),('bhedāḥ','<b>aspects, divisions</b>'),('striyaḥ samastāḥ','<b>all women</b>'),('pūritam','<b>filled</b>'),('bhukti-mukti-pradāyinī','<b>giver of enjoyment and liberation</b>')],
 '<b>“Striyaḥ samastāḥ sakalā jagatsu”.</b> Every woman in every world is her form. This line is often quoted to explain why, in Śākta tradition, women and girls are honoured as the Devī herself (as in Kumārī pūjā during Navarātri).',
 'Every woman <b>is a form of the Devī</b>.'),
VP(C,'8–9',S('8','9'),'NĀRĀYAṆI NAMO’STU TE',
 sc('A clock face of moments and seasons around the Goddess, with a glowing mind in a person’s heart',GLOW(110,80,60)+'<circle cx="110" cy="80" r="60" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{110+52*math.cos(k*0.524):.0f} {80+52*math.sin(k*0.524):.0f} l{6*math.cos(k*0.524):.0f} {6*math.sin(k*0.524):.0f}" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(12))+DEVI(110,30,.35,False)),
 'You who dwell in the heart of every creature as intelligence, giver of heaven and liberation, O Devī Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. You who, in the form of moments, minutes and the other divisions of time, bring about change, and who are the power that ends the universe, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you.',
 [('buddhi-rūpeṇa','<b>as intelligence</b>'),('svarga-apavarga-da','<b>giver of heaven and liberation</b>'),('kalā · kāṣṭhā','<b>tiny units of time</b>'),('pariṇāma','<b>change, transformation</b>'),('uparati','<b>the ending</b>')],
 '<b>The refrain begins.</b> From here on almost every verse ends “<b>nārāyaṇi namo’stu te</b>”. Nārāyaṇī is the feminine of Nārāyaṇa (Viṣṇu): she is the power behind him, and the ground (ayana) of all beings (nara).',
 'Mind in the heart, time in the world: <b>Nārāyaṇī, namo’stu te</b>.'),
VP(C,'10',S('10'),'“SARVA MAṄGALA MĀṄGALYE”',
 sc('A temple lamp and a garland around the Goddess Gauri with three eyes, devotees bowing; the first words written above',GLOW(110,60,56)+DEVI(110,10,.7,False,'#fff')+'<circle cx="110" cy="48" r="2.5" fill="#c0392b"/>'+'<path d="M30 140 q10 -16 20 0z" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+person(176,150,'#ff8c1a','#c98b5a',.6)+T(110,152,'sarva-maṅgala-māṅgalye',11,'#c0392b')),
 'O auspiciousness of all that is auspicious, O Śivā, accomplisher of every aim, O refuge, O three-eyed Gaurī, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you.',
 [('sarva-maṅgala-māṅgalyā','<b>the auspiciousness of all auspicious things</b>'),('śivā','<b>the auspicious one</b>'),('sarva-artha-sādhikā','<b>accomplisher of every aim</b>'),('śaraṇyā','<b>the refuge</b>'),('tryambakā','<b>three-eyed</b>')],
 '<b>The best-loved verse of the Devī.</b> “Sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye śive sarvārthasādhike” is chanted at weddings, at the start of journeys, at the end of pūjās, all over India. If you learn one verse from the whole Devī Māhātmya, many would say: learn this one.',
 'If you learn one verse, <b>learn this one</b>.'),
VP(C,'11–12',S('11','12'),'SHE RUNS TO THE HELPLESS',
 sc('A frightened, poor person kneeling, and the Goddess reaching down to lift them, the three gunas swirling around her',GLOW(140,50,40)+DEVI(140,0,.6,False)+'<path d="M120 80 q-30 20 -50 40" stroke="#f2c79b" stroke-width="6" fill="none"/>'+person(60,150,'#888','#c98b5a',.8)+''.join(f'<circle cx="{170+k*14}" cy="140" r="6" fill="{c}" stroke="#111"/>' for k,c in enumerate(['#fff','#c0392b','#222']))),
 'You who are the power of creation, preservation and destruction, eternal one, abode of the guṇas and made of the guṇas, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. You who are devoted to saving the downcast and distressed who come to you for refuge, remover of the suffering of all, O Devī Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you.',
 [('sṛṣṭi-sthiti-vināśa','<b>creation, preservation, destruction</b>'),('sanātanī','<b>eternal</b>'),('guṇa-āśraye guṇa-maye','<b>abode of the guṇas, made of the guṇas</b>'),('śaraṇāgata-dīna-ārta','<b>the poor and suffering who seek refuge</b>'),('paritrāṇa-parāyaṇā','<b>devoted to saving</b>')],
 '<b>Cosmic and close.</b> One verse makes her the power of creation and dissolution; the next makes her the one who runs to save the poor and frightened. Both are chanted daily. The second is one of the most comforting lines in the text.',
 'Power of the cosmos, <b>rescuer of the helpless</b>.'),
VP(C,'13–16',S('13','14','15','16'),'THE MOTHERS PRAISED · I',
 sc('Brahmani, Maheshvari, Kaumari and Vaishnavi on their mounts, each with Narayani’s glow',''.join(GLOW(30+i*54,50,22)+MOTHER(i,30+i*54,10,.85)+T(30+i*54,154,MOTHERS[i][0],8) for i in range(4))),
 'You who ride in the swan-drawn chariot, taking the form of Brahmāṇī, sprinkling water with kuśa grass, O Devī Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. You who hold the trident, the moon and the serpent, riding the great bull, in the form of Māheśvarī, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. You who are attended by peacock and cock, holding the great spear, sinless one, in the form of Kaumārī, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. You who hold the supreme weapons, conch, discus, mace and the bow Śārṅga, be gracious, in the form of Vaiṣṇavī, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you.',
 [('kauśa-ambhaḥ-kṣarikā','<b>sprinkling water with kuśa grass</b>'),('candra-ahi-dhara','<b>bearing moon and serpent</b>'),('mayūra-kukkuṭa','<b>peacock and cock</b>: Skanda’s birds'),('anaghā','<b>sinless</b>'),('śārṅga','<b>Viṣṇu’s bow</b>')],
 '<b>The Mothers again, as one.</b> In chapter 8 the Mothers came out of the gods; in chapter 10 they merged into her. Now the gods praise each Mother as a <i>form of Nārāyaṇī</i>. Many forms, one refrain.',
 'Each Mother is <b>a form of Nārāyaṇī</b>.'),
VP(C,'17–21',S('17','18','19','20','21'),'THE MOTHERS PRAISED · II',
 sc('Varahi lifting the earth on her tusk, Narasimhi, Aindri with the thunderbolt, Shivaduti and Chamunda with garland of heads',''.join(MOTHER(i,30+(i-4)*52,10,.75) for i in range(4,7))+KALIF(190,0,.5)+T(30,154,'VĀRĀHĪ',8)+T(82,154,'NĀRASIṂHĪ',8)+T(134,154,'AINDRĪ',8)+T(190,154,'CĀMUṆḌĀ',8)),
 'Boar-formed, lifting the earth on your tusk; Man-Lion, fierce to slay the daityas; Aindrī, crowned, thunderbolt in hand, thousand-eyed, slayer of Vṛtra; Śivadūtī, terrible, of great roar; Cāmuṇḍā, fanged, garlanded with heads, crusher of Muṇḍa: O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you.',
 [('daṃṣṭra-uddhṛta-vasundharā','<b>who lifted the earth on her tusk</b>'),('trailokya-trāṇa','<b>protection of the three worlds</b>'),('vṛtra-prāṇa-harā','<b>taker of Vṛtra’s life</b>'),('mahā-rāvā','<b>of great roar</b>'),('muṇḍa-mathanī','<b>crusher of Muṇḍa</b>')],
 '<b>Even Cāmuṇḍā is Nārāyaṇī.</b> The gentlest names and the most terrible all carry the same refrain. The garland of heads and the lotus of Lakṣmī are praised in the same breath.',
 'Gentle or terrible, <b>the same refrain</b>.'),
VP(C,'22–24',S('22','23','24'),'LAKṢMĪ, SARASVATĪ, DURGĀ',
 sc('Three panels: Lakshmi with a lotus, Sarasvati with a vina, Durga on her lion, each glowing',''.join(f'<rect x="{4+k*72}" y="4" width="68" height="152" fill="{bg}" stroke="#111"/>' for k,bg in enumerate(['#fff3b0','#fff','#fde4e4']))+DEVI(38,20,.4,False,'#c0392b')+MOUNT('lotus',38,110,.6)+DEVI(110,20,.4,False,'#fff')+'<path d="M96 100 l30 -20" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="4"/>'+DEVI(182,10,.4)+LION(182,120,.4)+T(38,150,'LAKṢMĪ',9)+T(110,150,'SARASVATĪ',9)+T(182,150,'DURGĀ',9)),
 'Lakṣmī, Modesty, Great Knowledge, Faith, Nourishment, Svadhā, the Steadfast; Great Night, Great Māyā, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. Medhā, Sarasvatī, the Best, Prosperity, the tawny one, the dark one, the Controller: be gracious, O Ruler, O Nārāyaṇī, salutations to you. You who are the form of all, Ruler of all, endowed with every power: save us from fears, O Devī; O Durgā, O Devī, salutations to you.',
 [('lakṣmī · lajjā','<b>fortune · modesty</b>'),('mahā-rātri · mahā-māyā','<b>great night · great illusion</b>'),('medhā · sarasvatī','<b>intelligence · the goddess of learning</b>'),('bābhravī · tāmasī','<b>the tawny one · the dark one</b>'),('sarva-śakti-samanvitā','<b>endowed with every power</b>'),('bhayebhyaḥ trāhi','<b>save us from fears</b>')],
 '<b>“Sarvasvarūpe sarveśe”.</b> After naming Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī, the gods sum up: you are the form of all, Ruler of all, with every power. Verse 24 is among the most popular Durgā prayers: “bhayebhyas trāhi no devi”, save us from fears.',
 '<b>“Bhayebhyas trāhi no devi”</b>: save us from fears.'),
VP(C,'25–28',S('25','26','27','28'),'HER FACE, TRIDENT, BELL AND SWORD',
 sc('Four protections: the Goddess’s gentle three-eyed face, her blazing trident, her bell and her shining sword, guarding a child',
  '<circle cx="40" cy="40" r="26" fill="#f2c79b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+''.join(f'<circle cx="{x}" cy="{y}" r="3" fill="#111"/>' for x,y in [(32,40),(48,40),(40,28)])+TRIDENT(180,60,.9)+'<path d="M28 120 q12 -28 24 0z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111"/>'+SWORD(180,150,0,1)+person(110,150,'#ffd60a','#c98b5a',.6)+GLOW(110,110,30)),
 'May this gentle face of yours, adorned with three eyes, protect us from all beings; O Kātyāyanī, salutations to you. Blazing, terrible, most fierce, destroyer of every asura: may your trident protect us from fear; O Bhadrakālī, salutations to you. The bell that fills the world with its sound and destroys the vigour of the daityas: O Devī, may it protect us from sins, as a mother protects her children. Smeared with the blood and fat of asuras, may the sword shining in your hand be for our good, O Caṇḍikā; we bow to you.',
 [('vadanam saumyam','<b>your gentle face</b>'),('locana-traya','<b>three eyes</b>'),('pātu naḥ','<b>may it protect us</b>'),('ghaṇṭā','<b>bell</b>'),('sutān iva','<b>like children</b>'),('śubhāya','<b>for our good</b>')],
 '<b>Her face first.</b> Before any weapon, the gods ask her gentle face to protect them. Then the trident, the bell and the sword. The bell guards “from sins, as a mother guards her children”: one of the tenderest images in the hymn.',
 'Her face, trident, bell and sword <b>all protect her children</b>.'),
VP(C,'29',S('29'),'PLEASED, SHE HEALS',
 sc('The Goddess smiling and touching a sick person who rises healthy; behind, a figure falls whose desires turn to dust',GLOW(110,50,44)+DEVI(110,0,.6,False)+person(50,150,'#2a9d6f','#c98b5a',.8)+'<path d="M50 90 q0 -20 10 -26" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>'+person(176,156,'#888','#c98b5a',.5)+''.join(f'<circle cx="{170+k*6}" cy="{130-k*6}" r="2" fill="#999"/>' for k in range(4))),
 'When pleased, you destroy all illnesses; when angered, all the desires one longs for. Those who take refuge in you meet no calamity; those who take refuge in you become a refuge for others.',
 [('rogān aśeṣān','<b>all illnesses</b>'),('tuṣṭā · ruṣṭā','<b>pleased · angered</b>'),('kāmān abhīṣṭān','<b>longed-for desires</b>'),('na vipat','<b>no calamity</b>'),('āśrayatām prayānti','<b>become a refuge</b> (for others)')],
 '<b>A healing verse.</b> “Rogān aśeṣān apahaṃsi tuṣṭā” is widely chanted for health. Its last line is beautiful: those who shelter in her become shelters for others. Refuge spreads.',
 'Those who shelter in her <b>become shelter for others</b>.'),
VP(C,'30–31',S('30','31'),'THE PIT OF “MINE”',
 sc('A person lost in a dark pit labelled MINE, a lamp of discernment shining down from the Goddess',NIGHTSKY+'<path d="M40 160 L60 100 L120 100 L140 160z" fill="#1c1430" stroke="#555"/>'+T(90,140,'MAMATVA',9,'#999')+person(90,150,'#888','#c98b5a',.5)+'<path d="M160 40 L100 110" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>'+DEVI(170,0,.4,False)),
 'This slaughter of the great asuras who hate dharma, done today by making your own form many, O Ambikā: what other could do it? In the sciences, the scriptures, the lamps of discernment and the first sayings, who is there but you? Yet you whirl this world round and round in the deep, dark pit of “mine”.',
 [('dharma-dviṣām','<b>of the haters of dharma</b>'),('bahudhā ātma-mūrtim','<b>making your own form many</b>'),('viveka-dīpa','<b>the lamp of discernment</b>'),('mamatva-garta','<b>the pit of “mine”</b>')],
 '<b>Back to the start.</b> The king and the merchant could not let go of “my” kingdom, “my” family (Part 5). The gods name that trap exactly, <i>mamatva-garta</i>. She spins us in it, and she is also the lamp of discernment that shows the way out.',
 'She spins us in the pit of “mine”, <b>and lights the way out</b>.'),
VP(C,'32–33',S('32','33'),'WHEREVER THERE IS DANGER',
 sc('The Goddess standing guard among snakes, robbers, a forest fire and a stormy sea, the whole world in her arms',HIMA+'<path d="M0 140 q55 -10 110 0 t110 0 V160 H0z" fill="#2a6ab0" stroke="#111"/>'+FIRE(30,120,.5)+'<path d="M170 120 q10 -10 20 0 q10 10 20 0" stroke="#2a9d6f" stroke-width="4" fill="none"/>'+ASURA(80,140,.4)+GLOW(130,60,40)+DEVI(130,0,.55,False)),
 'Where there are demons, where there are snakes of fierce venom, where there are enemies and bands of robbers, where there is forest fire, in the middle of the sea: there you stand and protect the world. O Ruler of the universe, you protect the universe; as its soul you uphold it; you are worshipped by the Lord of the universe; those who bow to you in devotion become the support of the universe.',
 [('ugra-viṣāḥ nāgāḥ','<b>snakes of fierce venom</b>'),('dasyu-balāni','<b>bands of robbers</b>'),('dāva-anala','<b>forest fire</b>'),('viśva-ātmikā','<b>soul of the universe</b>'),('viśva-āśrayāḥ','<b>supports of the universe</b>')],
 '<b>A travellers’ prayer.</b> Forest, sea, robbers, fire: the dangers of every journey in old India. Verse 32 was chanted for safety on the road. And again the promise spreads: her devotees become supports of the world.',
 'In every danger, <b>she stands there</b>.'),
VP(C,'34–35',S('34','35'),'THE CLOSING PRAYER',
 sc('The gods kneeling in a row with folded hands before the gracious Goddess as dawn breaks over a peaceful world',SUN(110,130,24)+'<path d="M0 140 h220 V160 H0z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/>'+GLOW(110,50,40)+DEVI(110,0,.55,False)+GODS(150,.4,5,30,40)),
 'O Devī, be gracious; protect us always from fear of enemies, as you have just now done by slaying the asuras. Quickly bring to rest the sins of all the worlds and the great calamities born of the ripening of evil omens. O Devī, remover of the world’s suffering, be gracious to us who bow to you. O you worthy of praise by all who dwell in the three worlds, be the giver of boons to the worlds.',
 [('ari-bhīti','<b>fear of enemies</b>'),('pāpāni sarva-jagatām','<b>the sins of all the worlds</b>'),('praśamam naya','<b>bring to rest</b>'),('mahā-upasarga','<b>great calamities</b>'),('varadā bhava','<b>be the giver of boons</b>')],
 '<b>For all the worlds.</b> The gods’ last request is for everyone: calm the sins and calamities of <i>all</i> worlds. Many read this verse as a prayer for peace in times of war, famine and epidemic.',
 'The last prayer is <b>for all the worlds</b>.'),
VP(C,'37–42',S('37','39','41','42'),'“I WILL BE BORN AGAIN”',
 sc('The Goddess granting a boon; a vision of a baby girl in the cowherd Nanda’s house and the Vindhya mountains',GLOW(160,50,40)+DEVI(160,0,.55,False)+'<rect x="20" y="70" width="90" height="70" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111"/><path d="M14 72 L65 40 L116 72z" fill="#c0392b" stroke="#111"/>'+'<circle cx="65" cy="110" r="10" fill="#f2c79b" stroke="#111"/><path d="M50 124 h30 l-4 10 h-22z" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>'+'<path d="M120 160 L150 120 L180 150 L210 110 L220 160z" fill="#7aa86a" stroke="#111"/>'+T(170,156,'VINDHYA',8)),
 'The Devī said: “I give boons; choose one that helps the world.” The gods said: “Always calm the afflictions of the three worlds and destroy our enemies.” The Devī said: “In the twenty-eighth yuga of the Vaivasvata age, another Śumbha and Niśumbha will be born. Then, born in Nanda’s house from Yaśodā’s womb, dwelling on the Vindhyas, I will destroy them.”',
 [('vara-dā','<b>giver of boons</b>'),('sarva-ābādhā-praśamana','<b>calming every affliction</b>'),('vaivasvata antara','<b>the age of Manu Vaivasvata</b>, our own'),('nanda-gopa-gṛhe','<b>in the house of Nanda the cowherd</b>'),('vindhya-acala-nivāsinī','<b>dweller on the Vindhyas</b>')],
 '<b>A link to Kṛṣṇa.</b> The baby girl born to Yaśodā on the night Kṛṣṇa was born, swapped with him and seized by Kaṃsa, slipped from his hands as a goddess: she is Yogamāyā, worshipped as <b>Vindhyavāsinī</b>. The Devī Māhātmya foretells her here.',
 'She promises to come again: <b>as Kṛṣṇa’s sister, Vindhyavāsinī</b>.'),
VP(C,'46–50',S('46','47','48','50'),'RAKTADANTIKĀ, ŚATĀKṢĪ, ŚĀKAMBHARĪ, DURGĀ',
 sc('Four small panels: a goddess with red teeth; a goddess with a hundred eyes weeping rain; a goddess feeding the world with vegetables from her body; Durga slaying the demon Durgama',
  ''.join(f'<rect x="{4+(k%2)*108}" y="{4+(k//2)*78}" width="104" height="74" fill="{bg}" stroke="#111"/>' for k,bg in enumerate(['#fde4e4','#dfe9f6','#e7f4ea','#fff3b0']))
  +DEVI(56,2,.32,False)+'<rect x="50" y="20" width="12" height="4" fill="#c0392b"/>'+T(56,74,'RAKTADANTIKĀ',8)
  +''.join(f'<circle cx="{132+(k%6)*12}" cy="{18+(k//6)*10}" r="3" fill="#3b6fd0"/>' for k in range(12))+''.join(f'<path d="M{136+k*12} 50 v12" stroke="#4aa3df" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(5))+T(164,74,'ŚATĀKṢĪ',8)
  +DEVI(56,80,.32,False,'#2a9d6f')+''.join(f'<circle cx="{30+k*12}" cy="146" r="5" fill="#2a9d6f" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(5))+T(56,154,'ŚĀKAMBHARĪ',8)
  +DEVI(150,80,.32)+ASURA(190,150,.35)+T(164,154,'DURGĀ',8)),
 'In a hundred-year drought, remembered by the sages, I will be born, not from a womb, and look on them with a hundred eyes: they will call me Śatākṣī. I will feed the world with life-giving greens from my own body until the rains come: I will be Śākambharī. There I will slay the asura Durgama, and be famed as Durgādevī. (Before that, red-toothed from devouring Vipracitti’s dānavas, I will be Raktadantikā.)',
 [('dāḍimī-kusuma','<b>pomegranate flowers</b>'),('rakta-dantikā','<b>the red-toothed</b>'),('an-āvṛṣṭi','<b>drought</b>'),('a-yonijā','<b>not born of a womb</b>'),('śata-akṣī','<b>the hundred-eyed</b>'),('śāka','<b>vegetables, greens</b>'),('śākambharī','<b>she who sustains with greens</b>')],
 '<b>Goddess of the drought.</b> Śatākṣī weeps with a hundred eyes for the suffering, and her tears become rain; Śākambharī feeds the world from her own body. Śākambharī is still worshipped, at Sakrai and Saharanpur in the north and as Banashankari in Karnataka.',
 'In famine she <b>weeps rain and feeds the world</b>.'),
VP(C,'51–55',S('51','53','55'),'“WHENEVER TROUBLE ARISES”',
 sc('The Goddess as a terrifying Bhima on a snowy mountain, and as Bhramari, a vast swarm of bees, with the closing promise written below',HIMA+DEVI(60,0,.55,False,'#2b2140','#3a3a5a')+''.join(f'<text x="{120+(k%6)*16}" y="{30+(k//6)*16}" font-size="12">🐝</text>' for k in range(24))+T(110,150,'yadā yadā … tadā tadā',12,'#c0392b')),
 'On the Himālaya, in a terrible form, I will devour rākṣasas to save the sages; they will praise me as Bhīmādevī. When the asura Aruṇa afflicts the three worlds, I will become a swarm of countless bees and slay him; I will be praised as Bhrāmarī. Thus, whenever trouble arises from the dānavas, I will descend and destroy the enemies.',
 [('bhīmā','<b>the terrible one</b>'),('bhrāmara','<b>a bee</b>'),('asaṅkhyeya-ṣaṭpada','<b>countless six-legged ones</b>'),('yadā yadā … tadā tadā','<b>whenever … then</b>'),('avatīrya','<b>descending</b>: avatāra')],
 '<b>Her avatāra promise.</b> “Yadā yadā bādhā … tadā tadā avatīryāham”: whenever trouble arises, I will descend. Compare Kṛṣṇa in the Gītā, “yadā yadā hi dharmasya…”. The Devī Māhātmya gives the Goddess her own promise of avatāras, age after age.',
 '“Whenever trouble arises, <b>I will descend</b>.”'),
]
for v in V[:15]: v['lab']+=' · '+HY
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="The gods led by Agni praising the Goddess, their faces like lotuses, the Seven Mothers in a ring around her, the dawn sun rising behind">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#3a1414" rx="8"/>'+SUN(190,230,60)+GLOW(190,90,90)+DEVI(190,20,.9,False,'#fff')+''.join(MOTHER(i,190+140*math.cos(math.pi+i*math.pi/6),160+100*math.sin(math.pi+i*math.pi/6)-40,.4) for i in range(7))+''.join(GOD(60+k*65,262,.55,*c) for k,c in enumerate([('#ff8c1a','#e0a800'),('#ffd60a','#c98b5a'),('#fff','#9fd3f0'),('#2a9d6f','#c98b5a'),('#c0392b','#c98b5a')]))+'</svg>')
P=dict(n=16,ch=11,out='/home/claude/work/devim/Devi_Mahatmya_Part16.html',
 title='The Nārāyaṇī Stuti · Part 16 · Devī Māhātmya series · srini.presents',
 desc='Devī Māhātmya, Part 16: chapter 11, the Nārāyaṇī stuti. The gods’ last great hymn, “sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye”, and the Goddess’s promise to return as Vindhyavāsinī, Raktadantikā, Śatākṣī, Śākambharī, Durgā, Bhīmā and Bhrāmarī. Explained in plain English with illustrations.',
 h1='NĀRĀYAṆĪ STUTI',series='DEVĪ MĀHĀTMYA · PART 16 · CHAPTER 11',tag='“Sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye” and the promise to return · explained and illustrated',cover=cover,
 how='The whole hymn (ślokas 2–35) <b>in full for chanting</b>, then a page for each verse or group, and the Goddess’s boon: the forms in which she promises to return.',
 where=[f'In {plink(15)}, Śumbha fell. The Goddess said “I alone exist”, the Mothers merged into her, and the whole world became healthy and clear.',
  'Chapter 11 is the gods’ last and greatest hymn, the <b>Nārāyaṇī stuti</b>, with its refrain “nārāyaṇi namo’stu te” and its most famous verse, <b>“sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye”</b>. Then the Devī gives a boon: she will come again, in new forms, whenever the world is in trouble.'],
 wtalk=("Why is she called Nārāyaṇī here?","Nārāyaṇa is Viṣṇu; Nārāyaṇī is his power, and the refuge of all beings. After the last battle, the gods praise every form they have seen, Brahmāṇī to Cāmuṇḍā, with one name."),
 fulls=[('THE HYMN IN ONE GO','Chapter 11, ślokas 2–9',HYMN[:8]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Ślokas 10–28',HYMN[8:27]),('IN ONE GO · CONTINUED','Ślokas 29–35',HYMN[27:])],
 fulltoc='The Hymn in One Go',
 fullnote='Text as given in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in (55 ślokas in chapter 11); its numbering is followed.',
 seq=V,
 toc=[('THE HYMN · ŚLOKAS 2–35',V[:15]),('THE BOON · ŚLOKAS 36–55',V[15:])],
 end=['<b>Prasīda (11.2–7).</b> Led by Agni, the gods beg her grace: earth and water, Viṣṇu’s power, Māyā and liberation, every woman and every science.',
  '<b>Nārāyaṇī (11.8–12).</b> Mind in the heart, time in the world; “sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye”; power of the cosmos and rescuer of the helpless.',
  '<b>Every form (11.13–24).</b> Each Mother praised as Nārāyaṇī; Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, Durgā: “save us from fears”.',
  '<b>Protect us (11.25–35).</b> Face, trident, bell and sword; pleased, she heals; she lights the way out of the pit of “mine”; calm the calamities of all worlds.',
  '<b>The promise (11.37–55).</b> She will return as Vindhyavāsinī, Raktadantikā, Śatākṣī, Śākambharī, Durgā, Bhīmā and Bhrāmarī: “whenever trouble arises, I will descend.”'],
 endsub='Chapter 11 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",'Part 17: chapter 12. The Devī herself tells the fruits of reciting her story: where it is read, she never leaves; on the eighth, ninth and fourteenth days, in Navarātri, in danger of every kind.'),
 endkey='<b>“sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye”: she is the auspiciousness in all that is auspicious</b>.',
 foot='Study notes on the Devī Māhātmya, chapter 11 (Mārkaṇḍeya Purāṇa), the Nārāyaṇī stuti. The hymn is given in full and other ślokas as in the Devīmāhātmyam edition of devimahatmyam.aurobharati.in, whose numbering is followed. Temple locations for Śākambharī are from living tradition. Meanings are our own plain-English renderings. Zappy and Prof. Bolt are fictional.')
build(P)
